mira_csm: Heads-up — onboarding with NookDesk. Their Electron desktop app is failing onboarding: a flurry of 429s from /v1/sessions during app start. They say background renderer processes each call SDK init.
kai_integration: account=nookdesk-prod, hosted. any response body or headers?
mira_csm: customer pasted minimal body: {"error":"rate_limited","hint":"preflight_concurrency"}. They also suspect our edge proxy is stripping custom headers.
oliver_sre: interesting. Electron has main+renderer processes; if each renderer fetches ephemeral token separately and then preflights, that explains concurrency. Also if a corporate proxy/NAT collapses connections, our per-ip heuristics might misclassify.
jess_devex: ask them to add an install-id and instance role header from the main process and let renderers reuse token via IPC. sending a tiny pattern for main process token caching.
kai_integration: also important: capture full response headers — we look for X-Redwood-Limiter / Retry-After. If headers missing, they may be lost at proxy or sanitized.
mira_csm: here's the sample main-process snippet we suggested (pseudocode): ```js
// main.js (Electron)
let cachedToken = null
let tokenExp = 0
const {ipcMain} = require('electron')

async function getToken() {
  if (Date.now() < tokenExp && cachedToken) return cachedToken
  const r = await fetch('https://auth.nookdesk.com/ephemeral')
  cachedToken = await r.text()
  tokenExp = Date.now() + 45000
  return cachedToken
}

ipcMain.handle('sdk-preflight', async (evt, args) => {
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.random()*200)) // jitter
  const t = await getToken()
  return fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/sessions', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${t}`,
      'x-install-id': args.installId,
      'x-instance-role': 'desktop-main'
    }
  })
})
```
